## The Full Chain End to End

```
TF creates: vault-unseal IAM user (no IAM policy)
TF creates: access key + secret key for that user
TF creates: KMS key with policy allowing vault-unseal user to encrypt/decrypt
TF stores: access key + secret into AWS Secrets Manager (same module, same state file)
  → GH workflow fetches from Secrets Manager
  → exports into SSH shell as env vars
  → Ansible lookup('env') reads them
  → template renders into /etc/vault.d/vault.env (protected by when condition)
  → Vault reads vault.env at startup → gets AWS credentials
  → Vault calls KMS Decrypt with encrypted master key blob
  → KMS checks key policy: is vault-unseal user allowed? YES
  → KMS returns decrypted master key
  → Vault unseals, storage accessible
```

Kept the IAM user, access key, KMS key, and Secrets Manager secret all in one TF module to avoid
data source imports across state files. Everything in the same tfstate references each other directly.

